INT. BOWLING ALLEY OFFICE - DAY

An open-plan office built into a defunct bowling alley. Desks sit on the lanes. Monitors glow where the pins used to stand. A scoreboard overhead reads "SYNERGY: 0."

BRANDYN HOLLIS, forties, a regional vice president in a vest with a lanyard, stands at lane three, clapping.

CHIDI OBI, thirties, an analyst with tired eyes, sits at lane four.

MARGUERITE FENN, fifties, head of compliance, in a cardigan, clutches a binder.

DEV PATEL-ROSS, twenties, an intern, kneels on lane two plugging in a power strip.

UNDERWOOD, sixties, the janitor, mops the gutter without comment.

BRANDYN
Team! We are pivoting to a bowling-first culture.

CHIDI
Pivot to what? We have no pins.

BRANDYN
Exactly. Pins are legacy.

MARGUERITE
Brandyn, the fire marshal says the lanes are not rated for desks.

BRANDYN
Marguerite, the lanes are a metaphor.

DEV
(from the floor)
There's a ball in my power strip.

CHIDI
The ball returns are still live.

BRANDYN
Great. Let's leverage the returns. Everyone grab a ball and a quarterly goal.

MARGUERITE
That is a safety violation.

BRANDYN
It is a culture violation not to.

CHIDI
Brandyn, I haven't been paid since the third frame.

BRANDYN
Strike, Chidi. We're all in a gutter right now.

A ball rolls down lane two and takes out Dev's power strip. The monitors go dark. The scoreboard flickers: "SYNERGY: 1."

UNDERWOOD
(not looking up)
Told you.
